      <p>DogsBay XML runs on Linux, macOS and Windows.</p>
      <h2 id="installing-from-a-package" class="group scroll-mt-20">Installing from a package<a href="#installing-from-a-package" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p>Each installer bundles its own Java runtime, so no separate JDK is required.</p>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Download the installer for your platform</strong> Go to the <a href="https://github.com/dogsbay/dogsbay-xml/releases">releases page</a> and download the <code>.deb</code> or <code>.rpm</code> package for Linux, the <code>.dmg</code> for macOS, or the Windows installer.
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Install it the usual way for your platform</strong> On Linux, install the package with your package manager. On macOS, open the disk image and drag the application to Applications. On Windows, run the installer.
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Start the editor</strong> The Welcome tab opens. From there you can open the sample project, which is where the <a href="./tutorial">tutorial</a> starts.
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <h2 id="the-command-line" class="group scroll-mt-20">The command line<a href="#the-command-line" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p>The installers put a <code>dogsbay-xml</code> executable beside the editor, so the command line needs nothing else installed — not a JDK, and not the editor running.</p>
      <p>Add its directory to your <code>PATH</code> to run it by name:</p>
      <Tabs defaultValue="Linux" class="my-4">
        <TabsList>
          <TabsTrigger value="Linux">Linux</TabsTrigger>
          <TabsTrigger value="macOS">macOS</TabsTrigger>
